# .publishignore — tracked files that must NOT ride along into the public
# export. One shell glob per line, matched against paths from `git ls-files`.
# Blank lines and #-comments ignored. See scripts/publish-export.sh.
#
# This is for things that are legitimately tracked privately but are not part
# of the public product: internal decision records, private notes, anything
# that is ours rather than the tool's.
#
# It is NOT a substitute for scrub-check.sh. Excluding a file from the export
# does nothing about the same content sitting in an earlier commit — which is
# why the export is squashed to a single commit with no history.

# Internal-only plumbing: private CI (a public repo gets GitHub Actions, not
# this) and the internal platform-standard marker files (gate config, repo
# kind, SDK ref, module exemption) — platform-conform tooling on the
# maintainer's machine reads these; none of it is part of the product.
.gitea/**
.platform-module-exempt
.platform-gate
.platform-kind
.platform-sdk-ref

# Decided (2026-09-17): stays private. docs/COMPLIANCE_MAP.md is go-to-market
# pitch language that contradicts the settled positioning (a recovery GATE,
# not a compliance/posture tool) and references a `restoregap evidence
# --framework` flag and a `compliance/controls.yaml` file that do not exist.
docs/COMPLIANCE_MAP.md

# Platform-internal conformance marker (livehost test allowlist), not part of the product.
.platform-allow-livehost-tests

# Internal launch tooling: readiness bars and probes for OUR launch, not the
# product. launch-bar.sh's grep patterns literally contain the identity
# strings the scrub gate hunts (found 2026-08-18 when an export FAILed on it).
LAUNCH_BAR.md
scripts/launch-bar.sh

# Internal findings backlog (non-blocking gaps noticed while prepping the
# repo for strangers) — same reasoning as LAUNCH_BAR.md: about our launch,
# not the product.
POST_LAUNCH.md

# internal launch prep (numbers re-verification, launch-day checklist) — never exported
docs/launch/*
